/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Z = PV/RT is 1 for an ideal gas at every pressure.
   - Z is PV/(RT); V is molar volume in L/mol; P is pressure in bar; B is the second virial coefficient in L/mol; C is the third virial coefficient in L²/mol²; B' and C' are pressure-series coefficients with units 1/bar and 1/bar².
   - B comes from pair interactions, C from three-body interactions.
   - The van der Waals equation replaces the ideal-gas pressure RT/V with two terms:
   - P is pressure in bar; V is molar volume in L/mol; T is temperature in K; R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ when V is in m³/mol and P is in Pa, or R = 0.08314 L bar mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ when V is in L/mol and P is in bar; b is the excluded volume per mole in L/mol
   - RT/(V − b) is the pressure of hard molecules of finite size; b is the excluded volume per mole, about four times the molecules' own volume.
   - At the critical point the T = Tc isotherm has a horizontal inflection.
   - Tc is critical temperature in K; Pc is critical pressure in bar; Vc is critical molar volume in L/mol; Zc is critical compressibility factor.
   - Real fluids have Zc of 0.23 to 0.29, so van der Waals overpredicts Zc and is poor near the critical point and for dense liquids.
   - Put P = PrPc, V = VrVc and T = TrTc into the van der Waals equation.
*/
