/* book Worked example 2: Q: The same CO2 is held at 300 K and 40.0 bar. Find its molar volume and Z from the van der Waals equation (same a and b), and compare with the ideal gas.
   1. Ideal gas first, as the starting guess: V0 = RT/P = 0.08314 × 300/40.0 = 0.6236 L/mol.
   2. Write the cubic: f(V) = PV³ − (Pb + RT)V² + aV − ab, with Pb + RT = 40.0 × 0.04267 + 24.942 = 26.649 and ab = 3.640 × 0.04267 = 0.15532. Its slope is f′(V) = 3PV² − 2(Pb + RT)V + a.
   3. Newton step: Vnew = V − f(V)/f′(V). At V0 = 0.6236: f = 1.4508, f′ = 17.064, V1 = 0.6236 − 0.0850 = 0.5385 L/mol.
   4. Repeat until the change is below 10−5 L/mol (table below): the root is V = 0.4996 L/mol. 300 K is just below the van der Waals Tc of 304 K, but 40 bar is far below its saturation pressure, so this is the only vapour root.
   5. Check by substituting back: RT/(V − b) − a/V² = 24.942/0.45697 − 3.640/0.24964 = 54.581 − 14.581 = 40.00 bar.
   6. Z = PV/RT = 40.0 × 0.4996/24.942 = 0.801.
   Answer:  */
