/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - For one mole, an ideal gas obeys PV = RT.
   - Z below 1 means attractive forces dominate, so the volume is smaller than ideal.
   - V is molar volume, Z is the compressibility factor, R is the gas constant, T is absolute temperature, and P is pressure.
   - The principle of corresponding states says that fluids at the same Tr and Pr have approximately the same Z.
   - Simple spherical molecules (argon, krypton, methane) follow this rule well.
   - Z⁰ is the simple-fluid compressibility factor, Z¹ is its correction, and ω is the Pitzer acentric factor; both Z⁰ and Z¹ depend on Tr and Pr.
   - Some syllabi call this the generalized compressibility factor correlation.
   - A generalized equation of state is written in Tr, Pr and ω, so it returns Z for any gas from three tabulated constants.
   - B⁰ and B¹ are dimensionless functions of Tr only; the group (B⁰ + ωB¹) equals BPc/RTc.
   - Z is then linear in pressure, so the correlation holds only where the real isotherm is nearly straight: low to moderate Pr (roughly Vr ≥ 2, or Pr below about 1 at Tr = 1.2).
*/
