/* book Worked example: Q: A gas has Tc = 300 K, Pc = 50 bar and ω = 0.20. At T = 360 K and P = 100 bar its generalized chart (Z⁰ + ωZ¹) gives Z = 0.61. Find Tr, Pr and the molar volume, using R = 0.08314 L·bar/(mol·K).
   1. Tr = T/Tc = 360 K / 300 K = 1.20.
   2. Pr = P/Pc = 100 bar / 50 bar = 2.00. These two numbers, not T and P, are what you look up on the chart.
   3. Ideal-gas volume: RT/P = 0.08314 × 360/100 = 0.29930 L/mol.
   4. Real volume: V = ZRT/P = 0.61 × 0.29930 = 0.18257 L/mol.
   5. Cross-check: the generalized Peng–Robinson equation at Tr = 1.2, Pr = 2.0, ω = 0.20 gives Z = 0.609, the same to chart accuracy. The Pitzer virial line would give 0.633, already drifting (see the figure).
   Answer: Tr = 1.20, Pr = 2.00 and V = 0.183 L/mol, about 39 % less than the ideal-gas 0.299 L/mol. */
