/* book Worked example 2: Q: Estimate the molar volume of n-butane at 510 K and 25 bar (Tc = 425.1 K, Pc = 37.96 bar, ω = 0.200) by (a) the ideal gas law, (b) the Pitzer virial correlation and (c) the generalized Peng–Robinson equation. Use R = 83.14 cm³·bar/(mol·K).
   1. Reduced conditions: Tr = 510/425.1 = 1.1997, Pr = 25/37.96 = 0.6586.
   2. (a) Ideal gas: V = RT/P = 83.14 × 510/25 = 1696.1 cm³/mol.
   3. (b) B⁰ = 0.083 − 0.422/1.19971.6 = −0.2323; B¹ = 0.139 − 0.172/1.19974.2 = 0.0589. Z = 1 + (−0.2323 + 0.200 × 0.0589) × 0.6586/1.1997 = 0.8789, so V = 0.8789 × 1696.1 = 1490.7 cm³/mol.
   4. (c) PR constants: m = 0.37464 + 1.54226 × 0.200 − 0.26992 × 0.200² = 0.6723; α = [1 + 0.6723(1 − 1.19970.5)]² = 0.8760; β = 0.07780 × 0.6586/1.1997 = 0.04271; q = 0.45724 × 0.8760/(0.07780 × 1.1997) = 4.291.
   5. Iterate Z = 1 + β − qβ(Z − β)/[(Z + εβ)(Z + σβ)] from Z = 1: 0.8808, 0.8619, 0.8584, 0.8578, 0.8577, 0.8576 (converged). V = 0.8576 × 1696.1 = 1454.6 cm³/mol.
   6. Pr = 0.66 is inside the Pitzer range, so (b) and (c) agree within 2.5 %; the ideal gas is 14 to 17 % high.
   Answer: Ideal 1696 cm³/mol; Pitzer virial Z = 0.879, V = 1491 cm³/mol; Peng–Robinson Z = 0.858, V = 1455 cm³/mol. */
